course: dictionary
lesson: 15746
title: A treater and trick-or-treaters
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A treater is a person who treats someone. To treat someone means to pay for them or give them something nice, such as a snack or a day out. «Dad was the treater, so he paid for our pizza.» Dad paid, so he was the one who treated us.
explain: The word treater is rare, so you will mostly meet it in one phrase: trick-or-treater. «The trick-or-treaters rang the bell and shouted, "Trick or treat!"» These are children in costumes who visit houses on Halloween night to ask for sweets.
explain: When you meet a rare word, read the words around it. Here, «Halloween night», «costumes» and «sweets» are clues. They tell you that a trick-or-treater is a child who goes from door to door at Halloween. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence.
fact: A treater is a person who treats someone, for example by paying for or giving them something nice.
fact: Treater is a rare word. You will mostly see it in trick-or-treater.
fact: «Trick-or-treaters» are children in costumes who knock on doors on Halloween night.
fact: Safety tip: trick-or-treaters should go with an adult, carry a torch, and only visit houses they know.
fact: Rare words are easier to learn when you know the phrase they are part of, such as «trick-or-treater».
exercise: «The little treaters ran from door to door in their costumes.» What is a treater? | A kind of tree | *A person who treats someone, for example by giving them something nice | A person who is always cross | A person who cleans houses
exercise: Copy the word that names the Halloween visitors: «Trick-or-treaters should carry a torch.» | carry | *Trick-or-treaters | torch | should
exercise: Which is a safe rule for trick-or-treaters? | Walk alone in the dark with no light | Go out after midnight | Visit any house, even ones you do not know | *Go with an adult and carry a torch
test: «Dad was the treater, so he paid for our pizza.» What is a treater? | A pizza maker | A person who is ill | *Someone who pays for or gives someone something nice | A person who hides things
test: «The trick-or-treaters carried torches and went only to houses they knew.» Why did they carry torches? | To wave at the cars | To frighten the houses | To make pizza | *To see and be seen safely in the dark
exam: Which word best completes this sentence? «On Halloween night, the ___ knocked on our door in dark costumes.» | *Trick-or-treaters | Teachers | Tree-dwellers | Treaty
